# Single-Cell RNA-seq Walkthrough

This notebook recreates the standard [Scanpy pipeline from the Sanbomics walkthrough](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvyG9yLuNSE) in BioLang. It runs on a synthetic 3-population dataset (T cells, B cells, monocytes), so there is nothing to download — swap the **Load** step for `read_anndata("your_data.zarr")` to use real data.

Run it with:

```biolang
# @skip
# BIOLANG_PATH=<repo>/packages  bl notebook sanbomics_walkthrough.bln
```

## Setup

We import the `singlecell` package and build a deterministic synthetic count matrix: 90 cells (30 T / 30 B / 30 monocyte) across 24 genes, where each cell's on-type marker genes are highly expressed. `MT-` genes give the QC step a mitochondrial fraction to work with.

```biolang
import "singlecell/src/mod" as sc

let gene_names = [
  "CD3D","CD3E","CD3G","TRAC","CD8A","IL7R","CCL5",          # T cell
  "MS4A1","CD79A","CD79B","CD19","HLA-DRA","IGHM","BANK1",    # B cell
  "LYZ","CD14","FCN1","S100A8","S100A9","CST3","VCAN",        # Monocyte
  "MT-ND1","MT-CO1","MT-ATP6"                                 # mitochondrial
]

fn gene_program(j) { if j < 7 then 0 else if j < 14 then 1 else if j < 21 then 2 else 3 }
fn cell_type(i)    { if i < 30 then 0 else if i < 60 then 1 else 2 }

fn synth_count(i, j) {
  let p = gene_program(j)
  let t = cell_type(i)
  let hi = 8 + ((i * 7 + j * 13) % 5)
  let lo = (i * 3 + j * 5) % 3
  let mt = 1 + ((i + j) % 3)
  let c = if p == 3 then mt else if p == t then hi else lo
  c + 0.0
}

let n_cells  = 90
let matrix   = range(0, n_cells) |> map(|i| range(0, 24) |> map(|j| synth_count(i, j)))
let barcodes = range(0, n_cells) |> map(|i| "Cell_" + str(i))
let truth    = range(0, n_cells) |> map(|i| cell_type(i))
println("built " + str(n_cells) + " cells × 24 genes")
```

## 1. Load — `sc.read_h5ad(...)`

We round-trip through native AnnData `.zarr` (pure-Rust, no HDF5) to mirror loading a real dataset, and record which backend served the load.

```biolang
let raw = sc.from_matrix(matrix, gene_names, barcodes)
write_anndata("pbmc_synth.zarr", raw)
let adata = read_anndata("pbmc_synth.zarr")
let _ = plan_backend("read_anndata_zarr")
println("Loaded: " + str(adata.n_cells) + " cells × " + str(adata.n_genes) + " genes")
```

## 2. QC & filtering — `sc.pp.filter_cells` / `filter_genes`

Filter on detected genes and mitochondrial fraction. Thresholds are small here because the toy set has 24 genes; real data uses e.g. `min_genes=200`, `max_pct_mito=20`.

```biolang
let filtered = sc.qc(adata)
                |> sc.filter_cells(3, 1000, 30.0)
                |> sc.filter_genes(3)
println("After QC: " + str(filtered.n_cells) + " cells")
```

## 3. Doublet scoring — `sc.pp.scrublet`

```biolang
let scored = sc.doublets(filtered, 200) |> sc.flag_doublets(0.8)
println("scored doublets")
```

## 4. Normalize + log1p — `sc.pp.normalize_total` + `sc.pp.log1p`

```biolang
let normed = sc.normalize(scored, 10000.0)
println("normalized")
```

## 5. Highly variable genes — `sc.pp.highly_variable_genes`

```biolang
let hvg = sc.variable_genes(normed, 15)
println("selected HVGs")
```

## 6. Neighbors + clustering — `sc.pp.neighbors` + `sc.tl.leiden`

BioLang runs a **real Leiden algorithm** (local moving → refinement → aggregation) on a symmetric kNN graph — the cluster count is discovered from the data, and every community is guaranteed internally connected. Exact labels still differ from `leidenalg` (RNG + graph construction), so we validate by *agreement* (ARI in step 9), not identity.

```biolang
let clustered = sc.neighbors(hvg, 15) |> sc.cluster_leiden(15, 1.0)
println("clusters found: " + str(clustered.clusters |> unique |> len))
```

## 7. Marker genes — `sc.tl.rank_genes_groups(method="wilcoxon")`

`marker_table` is a real Wilcoxon rank-sum test with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR (pairwise; Scanpy's default is one-vs-rest). We compare cluster 0 against cluster 1.

```biolang
let _ = plan_backend("differential_expression")
let markers = sc.marker_table(clustered, 0, 1)
let top = markers |> filter(|m| m.significant)
println("significant markers (0 vs 1): " + str(len(top)))
for m in (top |> take(5)) {
  println("  " + m.gene + "  log2fc=" + str(m.log2fc) + "  padj=" + str(m.padj))
}
```

## 8. Rule-based annotation

Score each cluster by the mean expression of each cell-type marker program and assign the winning label — the same manual strategy the video uses.

```biolang
fn program_score(mat, members, lo, hi) {
  mean(members |> map(|i| range(lo, hi) |> map(|j| mat[i][j]) |> sum()))
}
fn annotate(obj, cl) {
  let mat = obj.norm_matrix
  let members = range(0, obj.n_cells) |> filter(|i| obj.clusters[i] == cl)
  let t = program_score(mat, members, 0, 7)
  let b = program_score(mat, members, 7, 14)
  let mo = program_score(mat, members, 14, 21)
  if t >= b and t >= mo then "T cell" else if b >= mo then "B cell" else "Monocyte"
}
for cl in [0, 1, 2] {
  println("cluster " + str(cl) + " → " + annotate(clustered, cl))
}
```

## 9. Validate against ground truth (ARI)

Clustering can't match a reference element-wise, so we measure **agreement** with the adjusted Rand index — 1.0 is perfect.

```biolang
println("ARI vs ground truth: " + str(ari(truth, clustered.clusters)))
```

## 10. Provenance

Every backend decision was logged to `.biolang/provenance.json` — the reproducible record of what ran natively vs. in a container.

```biolang
println("provenance entries: " + str(len(provenance_log())))
```
